===== USAGE =====
bin2bmp file              # -> file.png, an image of the file's bytes
bmp2bin file.png          # -> file, the exact bytes back (or: bin2bmp -d file.png)

bin2bmp.py.png in this repo is the script itself, encoded with -s. Get it back with:
    python3 bin2bmp.py -d -o copy.py bin2bmp.py.png

===== OPTIONS =====
usage: bin2bmp <options> ... <filename>
-i, --input       	file to visualize (or the image to decode)
-o, --output      	output filename
-d, --decode      	get the file back out of an image (what bmp2bin does)
-w, --width       	width of output image (default 128)
-p, --png         	PNG output (the default; lossless)
-b, --bmp         	BMP output (lossless)
-m, --monochrome  	one bit per pixel, as a GIF (lossless)
-j, --jpeg        	JPEG output -- LOSSY: for looking at, cannot be decoded exactly
-c, --compress    	highly compressed JPEG -- LOSSY, as -j but much worse
-s, --sign        	store a SHA256 of the file, checked when decoding
-V, --verbose     	print lots of extra stuff
-v, --version     	print the version

Decoding needs no format flags: every image carries a small header with the file's length (and its
SHA256 with -s), so the exact bytes come back. By default bmp2bin writes to the image name minus its
extension -- normally the original file -- and warns when that file already exists; use -o to write
somewhere else. Images made by 0.1.x still decode (unsigned ones may keep trailing padding, since
0.1.x never stored a length).

JPEG is lossy: even at quality 100 it changes several percent of the bytes, so a JPEG is a picture of
a file, not a copy of it. Use PNG (the default), BMP or -m when you want the file back.

===== INSTALL =====
To install this program in Linux:
sudo make install
or
make install (as root)

===== RUNNING IN PLACE =====
There's no magic in the install script. On any platform with Python 3 and Pillow
(pip install pillow, or apt install python3-pil), just run python3 bin2bmp.py ...
(or bmp2bin.py, a link to the same script, to decode).

Since version 0.2.0 the script is Python 3. Since 0.1.6.5 it uses Pillow instead of PIL.
